Quote a real text at length (more than a few paragraphs, up to the whole piece) only if it is in the public domain, it is under CC BY, CC0, or the UK Open Government Licence, or it was written by Caspar Oesterheld (who is building this dataset). From any other text, quote at most a few paragraphs.

Don't default to public-domain classics just because their status is easy to check: recent writing is often a better fit. You can quote at length from:
- EA Forum posts published on or after 1 December 2022 (all CC BY 4.0, so the date is enough; many research organizations cross-post there)
- gwern.net and AI Impacts research pages, but not AI Impacts blog posts (CC0); Our World in Data, Distill, and Epoch AI articles (CC BY)
- arXiv papers whose abstract page lists CC BY 4.0 or CC0, and open-access journal articles whose page says "Creative Commons Attribution" (common in Synthese, Philosophical Studies, and Erkenntnis; the default in Philosophy and the Mind Sciences, Philosophy of Medicine, the Journal of Social Ontology, and the Journal of Ethics and Emerging Technologies)
- US court opinions and other US federal government documents, and Standard Ebooks' editions of public-domain books (public domain)
- UK government publications under the Open Government Licence, such as gov.uk pages and the International AI Safety Report; European Commission web pages (CC BY 4.0)
- Caspar Oesterheld's posts on casparoesterheld.com (not the guest posts by others)
These are not openly licensed, so quote at most a few paragraphs from them: LessWrong, the Alignment Forum, Substack newsletters (including Astral Codex Ten), Slate Star Codex, Cold Takes, 80,000 Hours, and the Stanford and Internet Encyclopedias of Philosophy. When you quote at length, say who wrote the text, its title, and where it appeared, as a real user would.

